WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.055
Welcome to Module 4 of the Azure
Security Engineering learning path:

2
00:00:05.055 --> 00:00:07.250
Private Link, D N S and Key Vault.

3
00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:12.688
A private endpoint gives a pass resource
an address inside the agency's network,

4
00:00:12.688 --> 00:00:15.325
and D N S decides whether anyone uses it.

5
00:00:15.325 --> 00:00:19.000
The secrets and keys a workload
needs belong in a vault that

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.975
only its managed identity and
eligible administrators can use.

7
00:00:22.975 --> 00:00:27.554
This module takes pass off the internet,
starting with Key Vault, and puts

8
00:00:27.554 --> 00:00:32.362
the vault itself behind role-based
access, purge protection and an audit log.

9
00:00:32.362 --> 00:00:37.591
It covers Steps 7 and 8 of the Azure
Security Engineering implementation guide,

10
00:00:37.591 --> 00:00:42.540
and takes about fifty minutes, plus
a lab in your own test subscription.

11
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:47.440
Why does this matter?

12
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.235
Learn's page, Integrate Key
Vault with Azure Private Link,

13
00:00:51.235 --> 00:00:55.352
is blunt about where private
endpoints fail: name resolution.

14
00:00:55.352 --> 00:01:00.156
The private D N S zone has to exist
and be linked to the virtual network,

15
00:01:00.156 --> 00:01:04.791
it has to hold the record, and
on-premises D N S has to forward to Azure.

16
00:01:04.791 --> 00:01:09.428
Miss any of the three and clients
quietly keep using the public address.

17
00:01:09.428 --> 00:01:13.148
And the vault: Learn's page,
Secure your Azure Key Vault,

18
00:01:13.148 --> 00:01:18.898
recommends role-based access, not legacy
access policies; pim for administrators;

19
00:01:18.898 --> 00:01:21.604
purge protection; rotation; audit logs;

20
00:01:21.604 --> 00:01:25.240
and private endpoints with
public access disabled.

21
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.926
So this module builds central private
D N S, turns public access off,

22
00:01:29.926 --> 00:01:33.079
and puts each vault
behind role-based access,

23
00:01:33.079 --> 00:01:38.120
with purge protection, a closed
firewall, rotation and an audit log.

24
00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:44.783
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

25
00:01:44.783 --> 00:01:48.670
You'll name the three things
private-endpoint name resolution needs,

26
00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.170
and say where the zones live.

27
00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.301
You'll explain what a private
D N S zone group does,

28
00:01:53.301 --> 00:01:55.258
and how policy does it at scale.

29
00:01:55.258 --> 00:01:58.190
You'll prove a vault resolves
to a private address

30
00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:01.408
inside the network and
refuses requests from outside.

31
00:02:01.408 --> 00:02:03.944
You'll check a vault's role-based access,

32
00:02:03.944 --> 00:02:07.907
purge protection, firewall and
public access in one command.

33
00:02:07.907 --> 00:02:12.061
And you'll read the audit log for an
allowed and a denied secret read,

34
00:02:12.061 --> 00:02:15.082
and say why disabling a
suspected key comes last.

35
00:02:15.082 --> 00:02:21.323
You need Module 3; Migration C of the
service account guide, Service Accounts,

36
00:02:21.323 --> 00:02:26.970
from User Accounts to gMSAs to
Microsoft Entra Workload Identities,

37
00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.536
for the managed identities;
and a skim of D N S,

38
00:02:30.536 --> 00:02:35.060
D H C P and I P A M, which owns
the agency's D N S authority.

39
00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:41.147
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 7.

40
00:02:41.147 --> 00:02:44.207
Create each private link
zone once, centrally,

41
00:02:44.207 --> 00:02:47.911
in the connectivity subscription,
and link it to the hub.

42
00:02:47.911 --> 00:02:52.623
The example is Key Vault's zone, from
Learn's Private Link integration page.

43
00:02:52.623 --> 00:02:56.985
Then create the private endpoint,
with a private D N S zone group.

44
00:02:56.985 --> 00:03:01.885
Learn's D N S integration page says the
zone group registers the A record with the

45
00:03:01.885 --> 00:03:06.710
endpoint and removes it when the endpoint
goes, so nobody edits records by hand.

46
00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:10.072
Once the private path works,
turn public access off;

47
00:03:10.072 --> 00:03:13.835
for Key Vault, the firewall's
default action becomes deny.

48
00:03:13.835 --> 00:03:17.152
On premises, add conditional
forwarders for the

49
00:03:17.152 --> 00:03:20.635
vault's public zones to
Azure D N S Private Resolver.

50
00:03:20.635 --> 00:03:25.669
And one exfiltration note: a private
endpoint reaches only its own resource,

51
00:03:25.669 --> 00:03:28.385
but traffic to it can bypass the firewall.

52
00:03:28.385 --> 00:03:32.159
Where inspection is required,
Learn's troubleshooting page shows a

53
00:03:32.159 --> 00:03:36.490
slash thirty-two route for the
endpoint's address through the firewall.

54
00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.302
At scale, let policy do it.

55
00:03:42.302 --> 00:03:45.006
The Cloud Adoption Framework's pattern,

56
00:03:45.006 --> 00:03:48.940
Private Link and D N S integration
at scale, has three parts.

57
00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:52.277
Deny private link zones in
workload subscriptions.

58
00:03:52.277 --> 00:03:54.466
Deny pass with public endpoints.

59
00:03:54.466 --> 00:03:59.015
And a deploy-if-not-exists policy that
adds the zone group in the central zone.

60
00:03:59.015 --> 00:04:01.346
The Azure landing zones initiative,

61
00:04:01.346 --> 00:04:04.920
Configure Azure pass services
to use private D N S zones,

62
00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.649
carries the built-in definitions,
and the policy's managed

63
00:04:08.649 --> 00:04:12.378
identity needs Private D N S
Zone Contributor on the zones.

64
00:04:12.378 --> 00:04:17.065
This prevents a danger the guide
lists: zones in workload subscriptions

65
00:04:17.065 --> 00:04:21.753
split records across zones, and
resolution fails or quietly goes public.

66
00:04:21.753 --> 00:04:26.421
The rollout follows the usual pattern:
the deny policies in audit first,

67
00:04:26.421 --> 00:04:29.112
with Module 1's public pass list reviewed,

68
00:04:29.112 --> 00:04:33.340
then public access off per
resource, then the deny policies.

69
00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:39.315
Unit 2 is the guide's Step 8.

70
00:04:39.315 --> 00:04:43.346
Managed identities themselves are
covered in the service account guide;

71
00:04:43.346 --> 00:04:45.328
this step covers the vault they read.

72
00:04:45.328 --> 00:04:50.040
Create vaults on role-based access with
purge protection, and close the firewall,

73
00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:53.627
with the commands from Learn's
Private Link integration page.

74
00:04:53.627 --> 00:04:56.602
Purge protection can't be
turned off once it's on;

75
00:04:56.602 --> 00:05:00.340
that's the point, and the guide
never excludes a vault from it.

76
00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:03.936
Grant the workload's managed
identity a data-plane role,

77
00:05:03.936 --> 00:05:08.627
such as Key Vault Secrets User, at the
vault's scope, not the subscription's.

78
00:05:08.627 --> 00:05:13.252
Administrators get Key Vault Administrator
as an eligible pim assignment.

79
00:05:13.252 --> 00:05:18.712
Rotate keys by policy, with Learn's autorotation
command; the periods in the guide's

80
00:05:18.712 --> 00:05:23.690
policy file are an example, and the
agency's key management policy sets them.

81
00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:26.860
And send the audit event
log to the workspace,

82
00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:30.115
as Learn's page, Enable
Key Vault logging, shows.

83
00:05:30.115 --> 00:05:35.080
Learn also recommends one vault per
application, region and environment.

84
00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:39.943
Two more things.

85
00:05:39.943 --> 00:05:42.255
First, a suspected compromise.

86
00:05:42.255 --> 00:05:44.697
Learn's page, Secure your Azure Key Vault,

87
00:05:44.697 --> 00:05:48.842
says to rotate and migrate dependents
before disabling the old key,

88
00:05:48.842 --> 00:05:53.505
because disabling it first takes
dependent databases and storage offline.

89
00:05:53.505 --> 00:05:55.467
The guide lists that as a danger.

90
00:05:55.467 --> 00:05:59.156
Back up the keys that can't be
recreated, and test a restore.

91
00:05:59.156 --> 00:06:01.205
Second, where agencies are.

92
00:06:01.205 --> 00:06:03.970
In commercial Azure,
which a G C C tenant uses,

93
00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:08.501
the zone names are the ones in the
commands: one central zone per service,

94
00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:11.803
such as private link dot
vault core dot azure dot net,

95
00:06:11.803 --> 00:06:14.568
and the vaults sit at
vault dot azure dot net.

96
00:06:14.568 --> 00:06:19.816
On other clouds, the guide names Google
Cloud's Private Service Connect,

97
00:06:19.816 --> 00:06:25.690
and O C I private endpoints, a private I
P in a V C N subnet for an Oracle service.

98
00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:34.303
The lab runs the guide's Step 7 and Step
8 checks in a test subscription where

99
00:06:34.303 --> 00:06:38.461
someone has applied them to a test
vault, with a private endpoint,

100
00:06:38.461 --> 00:06:42.540
public access off, a test secret
and a key with a rotation policy.

101
00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.840
The authors haven't run it,
and nothing in Azure changes.

102
00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.042
Step one runs on a Windows test
V M inside a linked network:

103
00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:51.740
look up the vault's name.

104
00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:55.051
The guide's Validate step says
it returns a private address,

105
00:06:55.051 --> 00:06:56.740
through the private link zone.

106
00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:59.702
Step two runs on your
own machine, outside:

107
00:06:59.702 --> 00:07:05.015
the lookup returns the public frontend,
and the secret request is refused.

108
00:07:05.015 --> 00:07:07.865
Step three lists the
endpoint's zone group.

109
00:07:07.865 --> 00:07:13.015
Step four reruns Module 1's public pass
query; the vault shouldn't appear.

110
00:07:13.015 --> 00:07:17.683
Step five checks the vault's
posture with the guide's own query:

111
00:07:17.683 --> 00:07:22.077
expect true, true, Deny, and,
with public access off, Disabled.

112
00:07:22.077 --> 00:07:26.810
Step six exports the key's rotation
policy, which is evidence.

113
00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:34.108
Steps seven and eight read
the same secret twice,

114
00:07:34.108 --> 00:07:37.035
on the test V M, so the
network allows both.

115
00:07:37.035 --> 00:07:40.931
First as the V M's managed
identity, which has the Key Vault

116
00:07:40.931 --> 00:07:44.910
Secrets User role on the vault:
the secret's name comes back.

117
00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:48.408
Then as yourself, with no
data-plane role on the vault:

118
00:07:48.408 --> 00:07:50.848
the request is refused as forbidden.

119
00:07:50.848 --> 00:07:53.934
The network allowed it;
role-based access didn't.

120
00:07:53.934 --> 00:07:56.960
That's the pair the guide's
Validate step asks for.

121
00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:01.535
Step nine, back on your machine,
finds both reads in the audit log.

122
00:08:01.535 --> 00:08:06.675
The guide's diagnostic setting writes to
the workspace's Azure Diagnostics table,

123
00:08:06.675 --> 00:08:11.210
and Learn's page, Monitor Azure Key
Vault, filters it by resource provider.

124
00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:17.223
Its result signature column holds the H
T T P status, such as O K or Forbidden.

125
00:08:17.223 --> 00:08:19.247
Expect a row for each read.

126
00:08:19.247 --> 00:08:23.319
Step ten: both az logout commands
signed you out of the test V M,

127
00:08:23.319 --> 00:08:27.350
and nothing in Azure changed,
so there's nothing to undo.

128
00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:36.155
The federal requirement, from NIST special
publication eight hundred fifty-three,

129
00:08:36.155 --> 00:08:39.525
revision five, as the guide's
requirements table maps it.

130
00:08:39.525 --> 00:08:43.806
Step 7 supports S C seven,
boundary protection, and A C four,

131
00:08:43.806 --> 00:08:48.087
information flow enforcement;
its evidence is the zones,

132
00:08:48.087 --> 00:08:52.550
the endpoints, and the public-access
state of each resource.

133
00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:57.597
Step 8 supports S C twelve, cryptographic
key establishment and management,

134
00:08:57.597 --> 00:09:01.682
S C twenty-eight, protection of
information at rest, and I A five,

135
00:09:01.682 --> 00:09:05.768
authenticator management; its
evidence is the vault posture,

136
00:09:05.768 --> 00:09:08.812
the rotation policies,
and the audit event log.

137
00:09:08.812 --> 00:09:11.548
Each row lines up with the fed-ramp twenty

138
00:09:11.548 --> 00:09:14.363
X key security indicators the guide names.

139
00:09:14.363 --> 00:09:19.304
The steps support these controls; the
organization still sets their parameters,

140
00:09:19.304 --> 00:09:22.830
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

141
00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.555
Check yourself with two questions.

142
00:09:28.555 --> 00:09:30.776
First: a private endpoint exists,

143
00:09:30.776 --> 00:09:34.230
but clients still reach
the vault's public address.

144
00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:35.593
Where do you look first?

145
00:09:35.593 --> 00:09:38.457
The answer is b, name
resolution: the zone,

146
00:09:38.457 --> 00:09:43.492
its link to the virtual network, the
record, and on-premises forwarding.

147
00:09:43.492 --> 00:09:46.542
Second: a key is suspected compromised.

148
00:09:46.542 --> 00:09:47.842
What comes first?

149
00:09:47.842 --> 00:09:52.502
The answer is b: rotate and migrate
dependents, then disable the old key,

150
00:09:52.502 --> 00:09:56.680
because disabling it first takes
dependent services offline.

151
00:09:56.680 --> 00:10:01.940
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

152
00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:06.515
To sum up.

153
00:10:06.515 --> 00:10:11.103
You built central private D N S,
with zone groups placed by policy.

154
00:10:11.103 --> 00:10:14.181
You proved the vault resolves
to a private address

155
00:10:14.181 --> 00:10:17.478
inside the network and
refuses requests from outside.

156
00:10:17.478 --> 00:10:22.953
You checked its role-based access, purge
protection, firewall and rotation policy.

157
00:10:22.953 --> 00:10:26.552
And you found an allowed and a
denied read in its audit log.

158
00:10:26.552 --> 00:10:30.599
The evidence an I S S O keeps from
this module: the zones, links,

159
00:10:30.599 --> 00:10:36.297
endpoints and zone groups; policy compliance
for the private D N S and public-access

160
00:10:36.297 --> 00:10:40.673
policies; the two lookup results
and the refused public request;

161
00:10:40.673 --> 00:10:45.958
the vault properties, role assignments
at vault scope and rotation policies;

162
00:10:45.958 --> 00:10:49.591
the audit event rows; and the
restore test when it runs.

163
00:10:49.591 --> 00:10:52.603
Next is Module 5: policy,
drift and evidence,

164
00:10:52.603 --> 00:10:57.310
holding all of it in place and
handing the assessor what they need.
